  "title": "Reedbank Market Gardens",
  "rules": [
    "Four actors per household have independent actions. Tool exchanges change who can plant, harvest, open gates and use boats.",
    "Opening a gate lowers local water retention and sends water downstream. Flood pulses provide fish/nutrients but can destroy ripe crops.",
    "Reed cutting opens boat passages, removes filtering plants and raises pollution.",
    "Empty-handed actors on opposite rack banks must both hold. This creates a real crossing and advances drying.",
    "Water/boat-tool holders can net opposite banks; one left-bank holder receives the catch.",
    "Neighboring seed diversity improves growth. Bird shelters protect their own and adjacent plots; carried reeds repair shelters.",
    "Fresh goods spoil after24 carried ticks; dried goods after64. Compost goods or sell them before the market preference changes each96 ticks.",
    "Role-local native state drives both policy and scene. Metadata does not prove a working human interface or animated replay."
  ],
